usb: musb: mediatek: program the toggle registers after phy_init()

mtk_musb_init() programs MUSB_TXTOGEN and MUSB_RXTOGEN before calling
phy_init().  That is safe only where something other than the PHY driver
has already ungated the controller.  On MT6592 the USB MAC and the U2
PHY are both behind the PERI clock gate at 0x10003010 and the PHY
driver's .init is what clears it, which makes those two writes an APB
access to a gated peripheral -- on MediaTek that does not fault, it
stalls the bus until the watchdog resets the board.

It has been surviving because the stock LK leaves the USB gate open on
its way out.  That is observable rather than assumed -- the J36 PHY
driver prints the gate register on both sides of its own write, and on
this board it reads

  PERI PDN0_STA 00000000 -> 00000000 (ungated mask 07ffffff); USB2.0 on

i.e. already clear before anything in Linux touched it.  But an open
gate at hand-over is an accident of one bootloader and not a contract,
and the toggle registers are only consulted during transfers, so moving
the writes below phy_init()/phy_power_on() is later than necessary on
every other SoC and too early on none.

WHAT THIS PATCH USED TO ALSO DO, AND WHY THE BOARD DELETED IT.

It carried a second and much larger change: a per-compatible variant
that gave MT6592 its own musb_platform_ops with .busctl_offset,
.get_toggle and .set_toggle left out, so that musb_core fell back to
musb_default_busctl_offset() and put the multipoint BUSCTL block at the
stock MUSB base of 0x80 instead of the 0x480 mediatek.c uses.  The
reasoning was that MediaTek added the TXTOG/RXTOG block at 0x80..0x87 in
the MT2701/MT7623 generation and relocated BUSCTL to 0x480 to make room
for it, that MT6592 predates both, and that the stock 3.10.72 kernel's
musb driver touches neither range.

The stock kernel not touching a register is not the same as the register
not being there, and that is where the inference was wrong.  The J36 PHY
driver was given a layout probe that settles it by writing to the
hardware -- a pattern to ep0's TXFUNCADDR at both candidate bases, read
back, restored -- and on this board it reports

  MUSB multipoint block is at 0x480 (0x080 read back 00): the MT2701
  layout, so linux/0003-musb-mediatek-mt6592.patch has it wrong and
  should be dropped

Both halves of that agree.  0x480 held the pattern, which only the
MT2701 layout can do.  0x080 read back zero, which is what MUSB_RXTOG
does when MUSB_RXTOGEN is still zero out of reset -- so the toggle block
is there too, exactly as the relocation implies.  MT6592's MUSB is the
MT2701 layout and mediatek.c had it right unmodified, so the variant,
the second ops table and the of_match entry for "mediatek,mt6592-musb"
are gone.  The device tree names that compatible first and
"mediatek,mtk-musb" second, and the second is what binds now.

That leaves the toggle writes unconditional again, which they should be:
this part has the registers.

Signed-off-by: MixOS J36 Ultra bring-up
---
diff --git a/drivers/usb/musb/mediatek.c b/drivers/usb/musb/mediatek.c
--- a/drivers/usb/musb/mediatek.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/musb/mediatek.c
@@ -264,10 +264,6 @@
 	musb->is_host = false;
 	musb->isr = mtk_musb_interrupt;
 
-	/* Set TX/RX toggle enable */
-	musb_writew(musb->mregs, MUSB_TXTOGEN, MTK_TOGGLE_EN);
-	musb_writew(musb->mregs, MUSB_RXTOGEN, MTK_TOGGLE_EN);
-
 	if (musb->port_mode == MUSB_OTG) {
 		ret = mtk_otg_switch_init(glue);
 		if (ret)
@@ -284,6 +280,18 @@
 
 	phy_set_mode(glue->phy, glue->phy_mode);
 
+	/*
+	 * Set TX/RX toggle enable -- after phy_init(), not before it.  On a SoC
+	 * whose USB PHY driver is also what ungates the controller's clock,
+	 * and MT6592 is one, a MUSB register access issued ahead of phy_init()
+	 * is an APB access to a gated peripheral: on MediaTek that does not
+	 * fault, it stalls the bus until the watchdog resets the board.  The
+	 * toggle registers are only read during transfers, so programming them
+	 * here instead is later than needed on every SoC and too early on none.
+	 */
+	musb_writew(musb->mregs, MUSB_TXTOGEN, MTK_TOGGLE_EN);
+	musb_writew(musb->mregs, MUSB_RXTOGEN, MTK_TOGGLE_EN);
+
 #if defined(CONFIG_USB_INVENTRA_DMA)
 	musb_writel(musb->mregs, MUSB_HSDMA_INTR,
 		    DMA_INTR_STATUS_MSK | DMA_INTR_UNMASK_SET_MSK);
